[36m(PPOTrainer pid=502412)[0m INFO 03-09 04:21:43 ppo_trainer.py:455] [trace] step=45 reward=1.000 response_len=221 total_len=2308 preview='systemYou are ChatGPT, a large language model trained by OpenAI.\\nKnowledge cutoff: 2024-06\\nCurrent date: 2026-03-09\\n\\nReasoning: medium\\n\\n# Valid channels: analysis, commentary, final. Channel must be included for every message.\\nCalls to these tools must go to the commentary channel: \'functions\'.developer\\n\\n# Tools\\n\\n## functions\\n\\nnamespace functions {\\n\\n// Return the average molecular weight (Daltons).\\ntype get_molecular_weight = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return the monoisotopic exact molecular weight (Daltons).\\ntype get_exact_molecular_weight = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return the number of non-hydrogen atoms (heavy atoms).\\ntype get_heavy_atom_count = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return Wildman–Crippen cLogP (octanol/water).\\ntype get_mol_logp = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return topological polar surface area TPSA (Å^2).\\ntype get_tpsa = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return Lipinski H-bond donors count.\\ntype get_hbd = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return Lipinski H-bond acceptors count.\\ntype get_hba = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return rotatable bonds count.\\ntype get_num_rotatable_bonds = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return FractionCSP3 = (# sp3 carbon atoms)/(# total carbon atoms), Often used as a simple “3D character / saturation” proxy from 2D structure.\\ntype get_fraction_csp3 = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return Wildman–Crippen molar refractivity (MR).\\ntype get_mol_mr = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return total ring count.\\ntype get_ring_count = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return aromatic ring count.\\ntype get_num_aromatic_rings = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return net formal charge (sum of atom formal charges as encoded in the SMILES).\\ntype get_formal_charge = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return QED (Quantitative Estimate of Drug-likeness) as implemented in RDKit.\\ntype get_qed = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return heteroatom count (non C/H).\\ntype get_num_heteroatoms = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Analyze fused ring systems (clusters of rings sharing bonds) and report their topology, aromaticity, and heteroatom content. Distinguishes fused polycyclic systems from isolated rings. Useful for AMES mutagenicity (detecting PAH-like systems), hERG (extended flat aromatic surfaces), and general structural classification of ring complexity.\\ntype analyze_ring_systems = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Classify the ionization state of a molecule at a target pH using Dimorphite-DL. Returns a formatted string with charge distributions.\\ntype classify_ionization = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string,\\n// Target pH for protonation (default: 7.4, physiological).\\nph?: number\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Compute fingerprint similarity (Morgan by default) between a query SMILES and a list of reference SMILES. Returns a formatted string.\\ntype compute_similarity = (_: {\\n// Query SMILES string.\\nsmiles: string,\\n// List of reference SMILES strings to compare against.\\nreference_smiles: string[]\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Screen a SMILES against RDKit\'s built-in structural alert catalogs. Returns a formatted string of alerts found.\\ntype score_structural_alerts = (_: {\\n// Query SMILES string.\\nsmiles: string,\\n// Which alert library to use. \'all\' uses all available libraries.\\nalert_library?: "brenk" | "nih" | "chembl" | "zinc" | "all", // default: all\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Extract pharmacophore features using RDKit\'s BaseFeatures definitions.\\ntype extract_pharmacophore_features = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Accurately parse functional groups with attachment points, corresponding fragment SMILES, and atom ids in the SMILES string for better structure description.\\ntype describe_high_level_fg_fragments = (_: {\\n// The SMILES string to parse.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Test whether a molecule contains the given SMARTS substructures and count occurrences.\\ntype match_substructure = (_: {\\n// The SMILES string of the molecule. Do not pass in an ellipsis (`...`) or other abbreviation.\\nsmiles: string,\\n// Mapping of pattern name to SMARTS string.\\npatterns: object\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Find the maximum common substructure (MCS) across query + reference SMILES. The output is a single structure present in ALL provided molecules. To compare very different molecules, call this tool multiple times individually rather than placing all molecules in a single list.\\ntype find_mcs = (_: {\\n// Query SMILES.\\nsmiles: string,\\n// Reference SMILES to include in the MCS search.\\nreference_smiles: string[]\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Desalt a SMILES by removing salts/counterions and keeping ONLY the largest organic fragment. Use this tool when the SMILES contains multiple disconnected components separated by \'.\' and one or more components are likely inorganic ions/solvents (e.g., [Na+], Cl-, Br-, I-, [K+], [Li+], [Ca2+], [Mg2+], [NH4+], sulfate/phosphate, etc.), or when you want the parent (active) molecule for downstream property prediction.\\nIn aqueous/physiological conditions, salts typically dissociate; counterions are usually not part of the parent drug structure for many structure-based predictions.\\nTypical examples needing desalting: \'CC(=O)O.[Na+]\', \'[Na+].O=C(O)c1ccccc1\', \'CCO.Cl\'.\\nDo NOT use if the SMILES is already a single component (no \'.\') or if multiple organic fragments represent real mixture/co-crystal/combination therapy that should be kept. Output is the canonical SMILES of the retained parent fragment; smaller fragments are discarded.\\ntype remove_salts = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Screen a molecule against RDKit\'s built-in structural alert catalogs (PAINS, Brenk, NIH, ZINC, ChEMBL, etc.).\\ntype score_structural_alerts = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string,\\n// Alert library to screen against (default: all).\\nalert_library?: "all" | "pains" | "pains_a" | "pains_b" | "pains_c" | "brenk" | "nih" | "zinc" | "chembl" | "chembl_bms" | "chembl_lint" | "chembl_mlsmr"\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return fraction of aromatic atoms.\\ntype get_fraction_aromatic_atoms = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return aromatic atom count.\\ntype get_num_aromatic_atoms = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return max absolute Gasteiger partial charge.\\ntype get_max_abs_partial_charge = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return min absolute Gasteiger partial charge.\\ntype get_min_abs_partial_charge = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return max EState index among atoms.\\ntype get_max_estate_index = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return min EState index among atoms.\\ntype get_min_estate_index = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return Bertz complexity index.\\ntype get_bertz_ct = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return Balaban J topological index.\\ntype get_balaban_j = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n} // namespace functionsuserInstructions: Answer the following question about drug properties.\\nContext: Mutagenicity means the ability of a drug to induce genetic alterations. Drugs that can cause damage to the DNA can result in cell death or other severe adverse effects. Nowadays, the most widely used assay for testing the mutagenicity of compounds is the Ames experiment which was invented by a professor named Ames. The Ames test is a short-term bacterial reverse mutation assay detecting a large number of compounds which can induce genetic damage and frameshift mutations.\\nQuestion: Given a drug SMILES string, predict whether it\\n(A) is not mutagenic (B) is mutagenic\\nDrug SMILES: O=[N+]([O-])c1cc2cccc3c2c(c1[N+](=O)[O-])-c1ccccc1-3Please think step by step and use tools when helpful. Then put your final choice ((A) or (B)) after "Answer:"assistantanalysisWe must decide if a drug is mutagenic or not. The SMILES is a large aromatic nitro compound with multiple rings. It\'s similar to known mutagens: many nitro aromatic compounds are mutagenic due to metabolic activation to reactive intermediates. For example, nitrobenzene, dinitroaniline etc are mutagenic. The given compound is like a large polynitroaromatic with many rings and nitro groups; likely highly mutagenic. So answer (B) mutagenic.\\n\\nBut we should also confirm with simple predictions. Tools: e.g., get_mutagenic? But we don\'t have such function. We can approximate via known data: Many nitro aromatic compounds are mutagenic. The given compound is similar to known mutagens like dinitroaniline, etc. So yes, mutagenic. So answer: (B). Provide final answer with "Answer:".\\n\\nLet\'s produce final answer: (B) mutagenic.assistantfinalAnswer: (B) is mutagenic.'
